{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his youth a regency was established under his mother and later under the influence of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was crowned in 1153 and began to assert his authority over Tripoli. His reign coincided with a period of increasing pressure from Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his diplomatic skills and his attempts to maintain peace among the fractured Crusader states. He often acted as a mediator between the King of Jerusalem and other nobles. He was married twice first to Eschiva of Bures and later to Stephanie of Milly. His first marriage produced no surviving children. His second marriage was politically significant as it brought him control over the Lordship of Sidon. Raymond was a close ally of Baldwin IV the leper king of Jerusalem and served as regent during the king’s illness. He was instrumental in the defense of the kingdom and participated in several military campaigns. His leadership was praised for its caution and strategic thinking.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III faced numerous challenges including internal rivalries and external threats. He was briefly imprisoned by Nur ad-Din in 1164 after being captured in battle. His release came after a large ransom was paid. He was also involved in the controversial Battle of Hattin in 1187 where he commanded part of the Crusader army. Despite his efforts the Crusaders suffered a devastating defeat. Raymond survived the battle and fled to Tripoli. He died later that year in 1187 before he could organize a new defense. His death marked the end of a long and complex reign. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a capable ruler who tried to preserve the Crusader states during a time of great turmoil. His legacy includes his diplomatic efforts and his role in the fragile alliances that held the Latin East together.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 